690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120690 #edb/ref 1061 17508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cd915cb-1be5-57c4-9174-958e51421e23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120691 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120691 #edb/ref 1061 17509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f601bb80-6906-5220-b7c7-22b3ad93f773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120692 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120692 #edb/ref 1061 17510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120692 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a4d6c4c-5b51-5273-8870-97758393eeff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120692 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120693 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120693 #edb/ref 1061 17511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120693 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f391ffe0-615c-5d48-86e2-243cac8338e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120693 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120694 #edb/ref 1061 17512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120694 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65f64dc2-9935-58c4-a4d2-93ee03872a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120694 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121041 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that the null space $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ of a matrix $A$ consists of the solutions to the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.$ To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the matrix ![](images/70/702794c0129df672cc7c4cc65260c9bfa7e33804ea9f71bba048cf62142a0a18.svg) Computing the reduced row echelon form of $A,$ we get\n\n![](images/16/16d52c068c616e2eca149a8e81929a98c85726361d7ea2291ab314fe7567e4ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n![](images/bb/bb8743bd8eb072476af0e9e31c0215916beeeb82fc371348de1e6013b38810f8.svg)\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ are basic variables (corresponding to the pivot columns) and $\\color{red}x_3$ is a free variable (corresponding to the non-pivot column).\n\nTherefore, any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/6e/6e124e2c030f6cad72932d09211935b438f1189322ded40683a1f18618e35f3e.svg)\n\nSo, the set ![](images/24/2449d6abf866e2baa123e81c1d610940930c75c6b45ecf818d36c62191ef605f.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ generates the null space of $A.$\n\n**Note:** It might be the case that $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ for a particular matrix $A$ equals the so-called zero subspace $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ containing only the zero-vector. The basis of $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ is said to be the *empty set*, which is a set that does not contain any vectors. The empty set is denoted by $\\emptyset$ or $\\{\\,\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121041 #edb/ref 1065 3207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121041 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "892648a7-3b42-5d92-9592-1a8859cc6b0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121041 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343346 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343346 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343346 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8096549b-b28d-56f2-bf0c-43aeeefb6cd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343657 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343657 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343657 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a0c0bcef-73f9-5e54-9770-e74f125c7660" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343818 #edb/ref 1080 "B={\\,}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343818 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343818 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b44048ba-44bd-5f22-88ba-adf8064f6edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343865 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343865 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343865 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b93e9ea2-0907-54d7-9c77-4b186882df67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344159 #edb/ref 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344159 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344159 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dab925f8-7cc0-56e6-bade-9e8ede8bb6b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344913 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344913 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344913 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186344159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344913 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f161998d-b322-5952-9ff6-f21c3c0f3666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345022 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edf8482c-d2e1-5075-b974-a0f2a06f53e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345022 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=-5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are free variables. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4} \\\\ -5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428406 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428406 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: linear=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1856->1861. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428406 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050426 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428407 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428407 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: linear=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1856->3029. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428407 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050527 #edb/ref 17592186045436]}
